        "# Supervisor / Orchestrator-Worker Pattern Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` implements a supervisor of three parallel workers using `threading`. The lead decomposes a query into sub-questions, workers run concurrently on each sub-question, and the lead synthesizes. No real LLMs \u2014 the workers are scripted to simulate fetch-and-summarize."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Key structure:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- `Lead.plan(query)` splits a query into 3 sub-questions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- `Worker.run(sub_q)` returns a fake summary (could be any tool-using agent in production)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- `Lead.run(query)` kicks off workers in threads, joins, and synthesizes."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Run:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "python3 code/main.py"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Output shows the plan, the parallel worker traces with start/end timestamps, and the final synthesis. You can see the wall-clock wins: three 0.3-second workers run in ~0.35 seconds, not 0.9."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Run `code/main.py`, then modify the lead to spawn 5 workers instead of 3. Observe the wall-clock effect. At what worker count does spawn overhead exceed parallel savings in this demo?\n2. Implement a worker timeout: kill any worker that runs longer than 0.5 seconds and have the lead synthesize the remaining results. What observability do you need to know a worker was cut?\n3. Add a conflict-detection step to the lead's synthesis: if two workers return contradictory answers, the lead notes the disagreement rather than picking one. How do you detect contradiction without calling an LLM?\n4. Read Anthropic's Research-system engineering post. List three practices that this toy demo would need to adopt to run in production.\n5. Compare LangGraph's `create_supervisor` (legacy) vs the new tool-calling recommendation. Which gives you better control over what the supervisor sees? Why does Anthropic explicitly pass only sub-answers and not raw worker context into synthesis?"
      ]
    }
  ],
